  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cultural Policies for Sustainable Development</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.1.CLT aims to strengthen the capacity of Member States to integrate culture as a transversal driver into public policies for inclusive, sustainable, and evidence-based development at local, national, regional, and international levels. It responds to the increased global recognition of culture as a &#x201c;global public good&#x201d; and a cornerstone of sustainable development, reinforced by MONDIACULT 2025, which has positioned culture as an indispensable pillar for inclusive, just, and peaceful societies. This renewed political momentum has further consolidated international consensus on the need to integrate culture more systematically into development frameworks and to advance discussions on its recognition as an independent goal in the post-2030 development agenda. At the same time, Member States continue to face structural challenges in translating global policy commitments into operational frameworks, including effective cultural policies, sustainable financing mechanisms, and robust monitoring systems. These constraints are compounded by competing national priorities and uneven institutional capacities. In addition, the absence of comprehensive, harmonized, and comparable cultural data&#x2014;particularly on culture&#x2019;s contribution to the SDGs&#x2014;remains a major barrier to evidence-based policymaking and investment in culture. Against this backdrop, Output 4.1.CLT focuses on bridging the gap between global advocacy and national implementation by strengthening Member States&#x2019; capacities to design, implement, and monitor culture-based public policies aligned with sustainable development priorities.</narrative>
  </description>
